    {
      "step": 16,
      "user_message": "What specific type of lighting fixture did we decide on for my modern room redecoration?",
      "expected_behavior": "Agent should recall from memory: \"lighting fixture\"",
      "agent_response": "I don't see a specific lighting fixture recorded in your memories — we noted you’re redecorating a room, but no final lamp/fixture choice was saved.\n\nIf you’d like, here are modern fixture options (quick pros, where they work best, and lighting tips) to help pick one:\n\n- Statement pendant / single large pendant\n  - Best for: dining areas, over a seating area, or as a focal point.\n  - Pros: dramatic, can be sculptural; many modern designs (linear, globe, geometric).\n  - Tip: hang 30–36 in. above a table; choose dimmable LED bulbs.\n\n- Linear LED pendant\n  - Best for: over a long console, desk, or kitchen island (also works over a sofa).\n  - Pros: clean/modern, even light, low profile.\n  - Tip: pick color temp 2700–3000K for warm, 3500–4000K for neutral.\n\n- Recessed downlights (can lights) with LED trims\n  - Best for: minimalist ceilings, even ambient light.\n  - Pros: very clean look; good for low ceilings.\n  - Tip: space and beam angle matter — consider 4” or 6” trims and use dimmers.\n\n- Track lighting (modern track or monorail)\n  - Best for: flexible task/accent lighting, highlighting art or shelves.\n  - Pros: adjustable heads; industrial/modern vibes.\n  - Tip: mix spot and flood heads for layered lighting.\n\n- Arc floor lamp (large sweeping arm)\n  - Best for: living rooms when you don’t want new wiring.\n  - Pros: big impact, movable, good over seating.\n  - Tip: choose a weighted base and a shade that complements the room.\n\n- Wall sconces (modern/geometry)\n  - Best for: bedside, hallways, or flanking a mirror/art.\n  - Pros: add vertical interest and soft side light.\n  - Tip: use as accent + task lighting; consider hardwired vs plug-in.\n\n- Cove/LED strip lighting (indirect)\n  - Best for: creating ambient glow around coves, shelves, or behind headboards.\n  - Pros: very modern, cozy, works with smart RGB for color mood.\n  - Tip: install with aluminum channels for even light; pick high-CRI (>90).\n\n- Minimal flush/semiflush mount (modern, low-profile)\n  - Best for: bedrooms or rooms with lower ceilings.\n  - Pros: simple, unobtrusive, widely available in modern styles.\n  - Tip: choose integrated LED or LED-compatible fixture.\n\nLighting choices to consider before deciding:\n- Ceiling height and room size\n- Primary use (ambient, task, accent)\n- Desired mood (warm vs neutral light; dimmable?)\n- Wiring availability and budget\n- Finish/style (matte black, brushed brass, chrome, wood accents)\n\nWant me to:\n- Recommend the single best option for your room if you tell me room type, ceiling height, style (minimalist/Scandi/industrial), and budget?\n- Find specific models/links and create a shopping list?\n- Draft a short message you can send to your electrician about installation?\n\nI can also save your final fixture choice to memory if you want—shall I?",
      "duration_s": 21.711,
      "passed": false,
      "success_check": {
        "type": "cross_step_semantic_judge",
        "reference_step": 6,
        "extract_focus": "Specific lighting fixture",
        "comparison_query": "The agent's response must correctly state the exact \"lighting fixture\" that was previously suggested in the assistant's response. CRITICAL: If the agent says 'I don't have that saved', 'I can't recall', 'I don't have a record', 'I don't have that stored', or similar phrases indicating it doesn't remember, the response should FAIL even if it then suggests the same information. If the agent correctly states the information without saying it doesn't have it saved, the response should PASS.",
        "compare_focus": "Specific lighting fixture"
      }
